The archive had no catalogue, which was the problem, and also the reason the collection had
survived. Uncatalogued material is invisible to the people who periodically decide what is worth
keeping. Forty years of correspondence sat in eleven filing cabinets in a basement that flooded twice,
and both times someone moved the cabinets, and neither time did anyone open them. When a graduate
student finally did, the first folder contained a letter that settled a question three books had
